# EVERY SUPPLY WITH NOWHERE TO PUT ITS EXCESS THAT STATES ALL ITS UNSERVED SHARES, BESIDE WHAT ITS OWN
# NUMBERS SAY WENT UNSERVED. One row per examined layer, `violates` where the exposure
# exceeds what anybody admits.
#
# ⭐⭐⭐ THE ONE PLACE THIS MODEL MEASURES SOMETHING WITH NO INSTRUMENT BEHIND IT. Nobody
#    filed the exposure; it is the ceiling the filer's OWN demand and nameplate imply -- the
#    largest demand against the smallest supply. What could have gone unserved is at most
#    what the buffer can absorb plus what they admit went unserved, and the buffer here
#    absorbs nothing by their own measurement. So the whole exposure has to be accounted for
#    in the holder rows. ⭐ `absorbable` is now READ rather than assumed: it is a sum across
#    the whole buffer row and it is zero by construction of this population, where the literal
#    `0` in the sentence below was a claim about the capacity column alone.
#
# ⛔⛔ EVALUATED AT THE HIGH CORNER, ON PURPOSE. The two sides of a transition fit are
#    ANTI-CORRELATED -- the week demand ran highest is not the week supply ran lowest -- so
#    anything summed across the demand range reports a week that never happened.
#
# ⛔ AN UNSTATED UNSERVED SHARE SUSPENDS THE INEQUALITY, so those layers are absent rather
#   than passing. Inheriting a neighbouring rule's `exposure > 0` filter instead is a
#   different population, and it counted three rows where nine are checked.
# ⛔⛔⛔ IT INNER-JOINS THE UNSERVED TOTAL, AND THE OUTER JOIN IT REPLACES WAS INVENTING ROWS.
#    `coalesce(u.unserved, 0)` gave a layer with NO unserved holder a zero nobody filed, so six
#    such layers entered the examined set. All six read `exposure = 0`, so `0 > 0 + 1e-9` is
#    false and the rule passed on every one of them WITHOUT BEING ABLE TO FIRE. Nine examined,
#    three of them real. arithmetic/exposure_bounded.sqlc, the site this rule guards, inner-joins
#    and declares three; a guard and its site disagreeing about their population is the guard's
#    defect, and reports/coverage.sqlc was counting the difference as coverage.
-- every slack on the layer accounted for and empty, against entries/unserved_totals.sqlc.
SELECT r.rule, p.filing, p.layer, p.violates, p.detail
FROM      (
    :compose(checks/roster.sqlc)
) r
LEFT JOIN (
    SELECT e.filing, e.layer,
           e.exposure > e.unserved + 1e-9 AS violates,
           format('exposure %s, absorbable %s, unserved %s',
                  e.exposure, e.absorbable, e.unserved) AS detail
    FROM (
        SELECT x.filing, x.layer, x.exposure, x.absorbable, u.unserved_high AS unserved
        FROM      (
            :compose(layers/unabsorbed_exposure.sqlc)
        ) x
        JOIN      (
            :compose(entries/unserved_totals.sqlc)
        ) u USING (filing, layer)
        WHERE u.unstated = 0
    ) e
) p ON true
WHERE r.slug = 'exposure_unaccounted'
